From d023c38d78442029f1b440d9d7be4db353eb9b50 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: local <local@localhost>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 11:55:03 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 01/19] ROCm: gfx12 WMMA multi-row GEMV (EXL3_GEMV_WMMA)

The fdot2 multi-row cores spend one dot2 per row per weight pair, so an
8-row call (a DFlash2 verify) costs 1.4-1.9x a 1-row call while reading the
same trellis. The new kernel decodes each 16x16 tile once with the RDNA tiles
decoder, stages it through a per-wave LDS slot and runs one
v_wmma_f32_16x16x16_f16 over all rows (layout probed on gfx1201). An 8-row
verify drops to about 1.16x a 1-row decode (was about 1.55x).

- Opt-in with EXL3_GEMV_WMMA=1 (read per call); EXL3_GEMV_WMMA_U picks the
  k-tile batch (1, 2, 4).
- Integer bitrates, m 2-8, single-matrix GEMV and the unweighted single-token
  multi-matrix GEMV. Weighted (MoE) and multi-token mgemv keep fdot2.
- Gated per device at runtime: only gfx1200/gfx1201 with wave32 take it (the
  body traps elsewhere), so gfx11 and other targets are unaffected by the flag.
- LDS exchanges are fenced with __syncwarp (fence + wave barrier); the bare
  wave_barrier builtin does not order LDS accesses.
- Rows past size_m read row 0 and land only in output rows the epilogue never
  stores, so the kernel never reads past A (the fdot2 cores' equivalent fix
  is upstream's cc50bbb).

fp32 accumulation order differs from the fdot2 chain, so results match the
m == 1 path to rounding, not bit for bit.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
